# Python abstractions in a monorepo

- Determine package ownership and supported entry points from source, callers,
  packaging configuration, and project decisions. An __init__.py export may help
  identify the interface, but it is not the only possible public entry point.
- Use the owning package's supported operations. Avoid new cross-package imports
  of private implementation modules or names marked with a leading underscore.
  If existing callers do this, identify the contract gap before expanding it.
- Keep domain rules with the package that owns them. Do not move business behavior
  into a shared utility package solely to remove duplication or resolve an import.
- Follow established dependency direction. Avoid new circular imports, sys.path
  modifications, or import-time side effects used to connect packages. A justified
  local import is not automatically a defect; examine the dependency it represents.
- Reuse existing functions, types, and dependency-injection conventions. Introduce
  a class, Protocol, abstract base class, registry, or factory only when it solves
  a concrete contract or variation problem. Do not add them to improve a score.
- Make relevant inputs, return values, exceptions, mutation, resource lifetime,
  and sync/async behavior clear. Use established type-checking conventions; type
  hints alone do not enforce runtime business rules.
- Avoid new hidden module-level mutable state. Pass or encapsulate dependencies
  according to existing conventions when state ownership or test setup needs it.
- Test meaningful behavior at supported interfaces with the project's tools.
  Prefer explicit dependencies when internal monkeypatching masks ownership, but
  do not ban useful monkeypatching or replace established tests without a need.
- Run the affected package's checks and relevant consumer/integration checks when
  a shared contract changes. State what was and was not tested. Do not install a
  new linter or test framework simply to perform this assessment.
